IMPLEMENTATION CONTRACT


1. PRESENTATION BUILDS ON CODEX 08

Do not rewrite or independently reimplement:

- history
- draft preservation
- async guard
- IME handling
- focus behavior
- scroll ownership
- follow-tail
- viewport/editing geometry

Codex 09 changes presentation around the accepted interaction foundation.


2. SHARED GRAMMAR, DISTINCT SKINS

Share presentation rules only where both existing terminals demonstrate the
same concrete need.

Reasonable shared concerns include:

- input-line structural geometry
- safe wrapping
- selection behavior
- generic monospace constraints
- reusable spacing primitives

Keep OS-specific:

- prompt identity
- accent treatment
- chrome
- labels
- output personality
- Firmware character

Do not build a Terminal theme engine.


3. TERMINAL VISUAL HIERARCHY

Each Terminal should read clearly as:

OPERATING CONTEXT
↓
OUTPUT HISTORY
↓
ACTIVE PROMPT / INPUT

The prompt/input row must feel like part of the Terminal, not a generic browser
form attached below it.

Avoid card-per-command layouts.


4. OUTPUT DENSITY

Terminal output should be compact enough for real phone use while keeping
separate interactions understandable.

Use restrained spacing to distinguish:

- submitted command
- result
- process projection
- next command

Do not create large bordered result cards.


5. PROMPT + INPUT

Prompts remain adapter-owned.

NODE and RACK prompt semantics must not change merely for layout.

The input should:

- remain visibly editable
- remain integrated with the prompt
- retain sufficient usable width on narrow devices
- avoid browser-form styling

Do not:

- move the prompt into scrolling history
- hide important prompt identity without strong reason
- introduce whole-page horizontal scrolling


6. NATIVE EDITING CARET

While the actual input is focused, the browser/native caret is the real editing
caret.

Do not overlay a second fake editing cursor on top of it.

Any decorative idle cursor must:

- not imply false focus
- not affect geometry
- not interfere with selection
- respect prefers-reduced-motion


7. TYPOGRAPHY

Use the existing stack; do not add an external font dependency.

Prioritize readability of:

- commands
- paths
- IPv4 addresses
- ports
- release IDs
- punctuation
- version strings

Preserve the accepted mobile input sizing required to avoid iOS focus autozoom.

Output text may be denser than the command input where readability remains good.


8. LONG TECHNICAL VALUES

Long technical values must remain fully represented.

Examples:

- commands
- paths
- endpoints
- release IDs
- error strings

They must not create Shell/Page horizontal scrolling.

Prefer safe visual wrapping.

Do not mutate the canonical/copyable value to make wrapping work.

Avoid ellipsis for values the player may need exactly.


9. TEXT SELECTION

Normal Terminal output remains ordinary selectable text.

Do not apply:

user-select: none

to the whole Terminal.

A scoped selection color treatment is acceptable if it materially improves
readability.


10. TARGET TOKEN PRESENTATION

TargetToken remains inline technical content that happens to be actionable.

Preserve its accepted semantic/accessibility behavior.

Do not turn target values into large:

- pills
- badges
- cards
- raised buttons

Improve hit comfort and focus visibility only without inflating normal Terminal
line height unnecessarily.


11. COPY FEEDBACK

Copy success/failure feedback should remain:

- temporary
- local
- accessible
- non-canonical

It should not cause distracting terminal-line reflow or viewport movement.

Do not move clipboard execution away from the direct user activation.


12. SCROLLBAR

Where desktop/browser scrollbars are visible, they may receive restrained
Terminal-appropriate presentation.

Do not:

- implement a JavaScript custom scrollbar
- globally hide scrollbars
- interfere with accepted Codex 08 scrolling behavior

Touch environments may continue relying on native scrollbar behavior.


13. NODE-OS IDENTITY

NODE-OS Terminal should remain consistent with the accepted NODE-OS visual
language:

- matte dark
- restrained green/mint accents
- clean technical hierarchy
- low visual noise
- polished personal-system character

Avoid:

- neon glow
- CRT gimmicks
- scanlines
- fake static
- cyberpunk decoration


14. RACK-OS IDENTITY

RACK-OS should remain:

- more utilitarian
- more server-oriented
- visually foreign to NODE-OS
- less productized

But not:

- less usable
- intentionally awkward
- harder to read
- interaction-inferior

Do not obtain distinction merely by copying NODE CSS and changing one color.


15. REAL OUTPUT ONLY

Do not add visual atmosphere that implies nonexistent simulation state.

Do not invent:

- startup logs
- security banners
- uptime
- latency
- network traffic
- root state
- permissions
- background jobs
- shell environment state
- fake telemetry

Silence is valid.


16. PROCESS PROJECTIONS

Existing real NODE Process projections may receive narrow visual polish.

Only display canonical represented Process information.

Do not invent additional progress/resources/activity.


17. COLOR + ACCESSIBILITY

Color may reinforce meaning but must not be the only distinction.

Preserve visible keyboard focus.

Important text must retain usable contrast.

Do not remove accessible outlines without an equivalent replacement.


18. TOUCH FEEDBACK

Interactive elements may use restrained immediate touch feedback.

Avoid:

- bounce
- large scale animation
- layout-changing press effects

Interaction feedback must not disturb Terminal geometry.


19. MOBILE + SAFE AREA

Preserve accepted Shell editing and safe-area behavior.

Do not add Terminal-specific:

- viewport calculations
- keyboard-height calculations
- device-specific pixel hacks
- VisualViewport listeners

Portrait is primary.

Landscape must remain usable.


20. DESKTOP

Do not turn desktop Terminal into an oversized mobile layout.

Use available space sensibly while maintaining readable line lengths.

Do not add card framing merely to limit content width.


21. CSS OWNERSHIP

Put presentation rules at the narrowest honest ownership boundary.

Shared Terminal rules:
→ shared interaction/presentation layer where genuinely common

NODE-specific rules:
→ NODE Terminal

RACK-specific rules:
→ RACK-OS

Avoid broad global selectors for Terminal-only behavior.

Avoid specificity escalation and unnecessary `!important`.


22. NO NEW DEPENDENCIES

Do not add:

- terminal emulator library
- animation framework
- scrollbar library
- theme framework
- icon framework

Use the current stack.


23. COMMAND + GAMEPLAY SEMANTICS STAY FIXED

Do not change command meaning or gameplay results to make output prettier.

Do not add:

- Inspect
- aliases
- theme commands
- settings commands
- autocomplete
- new terminal capabilities

Terminal presentation remains an interface over accepted operations.


24. NO FAKE DURATIONS

Do not reintroduce fake waiting removed by Codex 08.

Animation must not imply work where no represented work exists.


25. NO GAMESTATE CHANGE

This is presentation work.

Do not add canonical state for:

- copy feedback
- focus
- visual cursor
- selection
- layout

GAME_STATE_VERSION remains the accepted value.

Expected:

16


